BOOT_A_LEFT=0
BOOT_B_LEFT=3
# cat /data/keep
marker

--- reading it --------------------------------------------------------------------------
Result: PASS. The previous version boots, the root filesystem is read-only, the data is intact,
and the half-written slot is out of the way: RAUC had marked it bad before the first byte was
written, so it is absent from BOOT_ORDER, has no attempts left, and rauc status calls it bad.
U-Boot never considered it.

The cut came about one second into the write (46% to 53% is about a second of an eight-second
copy), which is the "early in the write" point. The procedure had asked for "right after Marking
bad", a window of some 80 ms between that line and the first write, which a hand on a plug
cannot hit; the log above shows the order (Marked slot as bad, then Updating) and this trial
shows the state that order leaves behind. quickstart scenario 4 now names the points a hand can
reach.

Boot-time observation that is not part of the pass/fail: "PM: Cannot get wkup_m3_ipc handle"
appeared at 6.5 s in this boot. It is in the pre-feature baseline logs as well (boot-baseline-run1
and run4), alternating from boot to boot with "PM: bootloader does not support rtc-only!", so it
is a race in the PM firmware setup and not something this work introduced.
